WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.120
The science of black
holes is so challenging

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.700
that some scientists
question whether they

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:06.750
exist at all.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.310
Despite their fearsome
reputation.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.720
We've never actually
seen one.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.190
Black holes are
everywhere there, all

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:23.660
over the universe.
They're all throughout

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.610
our galaxy. But that
doesn't mean that they're

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:26.510
easy to find.

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:29.780
They're black and space
is black.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.840
And black on black is
kind of hard to see in a

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.160
picture of space.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.970
This is paradoxical
because scientists

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.910
believe black holes are
born and some of the

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.220
brightest explosions in
the universe.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:50.130
Rising from the corpses
of detonated stars many

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.300
times larger than our
sun.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.950
The star that burns for
10 million years

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:00.310
collapses to form a
black hole in a period of

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:01.330
seconds.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.940
As it collapses, the
outer region of the star

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.760
hits the core,
triggering a huge

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.220
explosion, a supernova.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.700
We see the bang, but not
what's left behind.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:27.000
A dead core with the
enormous mass of the star

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.170
crushed down into an
infinitesimal, tiny area.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.780
From this minuscule high
mass core.

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:44.210
A black hole is born.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.790
The flow of gravity is so
strong that nothing can

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.560
escape. Not even light.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.830
But how can scientists
claim that black holes

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.230
exist if we can't even
see them?

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:07.940
You could say that about
the existence of the

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.760
atom. We knew they had
existed for decades,

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:11.930
centuries before.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:15.350
We had actually seen one
in some sort of imaging

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:18.530
device. And so it's the
same sort of thing with

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:19.370
black holes.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.580
Just because you can't
see it doesn't mean it's

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.310
not there. Not seeing
black holes, but knowing

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.130
they're there is a
possibility, just like we

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:30.740
know that wind is there
even though we can't see

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:31.130
air.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.650
Air is invisible, but
when the wind blows, its

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.270
effects can be measured.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.110
It's the same with black
holes.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:45.290
You just need to know
what to look for.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.610
While they emit no light
themselves.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:51.550
Black holes are
tremendous sources of x

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.190
rays, and that's because
as things get close to a

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.380
black hole, they're
accelerated by the

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.420
gravity and they can
heat up to millions of

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:01.750
degrees. Million degree
gas gives you lots of x

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:02.290
rays.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:08.050
To find and measure these
telltale x rays.

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.640
Scientists turned to the
new Star Space Telescope

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.260
in 2017.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:21.130
It spots a burst of x
rays in a cluster called

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:24.760
47 to Cana at the edge
of the Milky Way.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.120
When scientists analyze
the data, they realize

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:33.880
they're looking at two
objects orbiting each

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.620
other very closely.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.520
All we see is that
there's a star being

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.910
ripped apart and gas is
spiraling down to a very

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:46.490
dense, very dark object.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.660
So something weird is
going on.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:50.490
As one of the.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.920
Objects accretes matter
off the other.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.040
It causes it to emit x
rays, and those x rays

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.380
can be used then to
trace out the orbits and

00:03:58.380 --> 00:03:59.400
therefore extract the
mass.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.440
When scientists work out
the size and mass of the

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.330
two objects they find.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:11.510
The first is the fading
corpse of a sun like

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:16.850
star. And while the
second object is tiny, it

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:19.040
has the mass of a giant.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.550
Is this an elusive black
hole?

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.300
What we're talking about
here is an object that is

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.840
very massive, very
small, very dense with

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.010
intense gravity.

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:34.730
But it turns out there
are lots of different

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.040
ways to create an object
like that.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.150
There is another type of
ultra dense object out

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:42.280
there in the universe
called a neutron star.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:50.070
Neutron stars form in the
same way we think black

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:54.090
holes form when stars
die, explode, but then

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:57.150
collapse down into a
tiny ball of matter.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:01.630
The gravitational
attraction of a neutron

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:05.860
star is enormous,
pulling in gas, dust and

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:09.130
asteroids. But light can
still escape.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.110
Black holes and neutron
stars are kind of

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:16.260
cousins. But in the case
of a neutron star, it

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.200
didn't have quite enough
mass to collapse out of

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.570
control. So you can sort
of think of it as just

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.300
barely hanging back from
collapsing into a black

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:24.690
hole.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.630
The tiny object
discovered by NuStar does

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.190
have enormous mass.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:38.280
But size and mass alone
are not enough to prove

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.330
it's a black hole.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.200
Cosmologists need more
evidence.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:46.770
They can't see black
holes.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:51.030
But is there another
way? What if they could

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:51.690
hear them?

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:56.050
Think of two massive cars
colliding.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:59.680
Boom. When they do, they
radiate sound and then we

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.480
can tell whether or not
that collision occurred

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:02.950
and maybe even how far
away it was.

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:04.840
It's like that when
black holes collide.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:14.240
So by listening for a
black hole crash.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:19.880
Could scientists
conclusively prove they

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:20.660
exist?

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.590
There are gravitational
waves going through this

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:27.810
room all the time.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:30.340
Every time I move my
hands like I just did, I

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:33.430
create gravitational
waves. The problem is

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.580
gravity is so weak that
you don't detect those

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:37.690
gravitational waves.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.200
In order to detect those
disturbances in space and

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:46.070
time, you have to have
cataclysmic events

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:47.000
involving massive
objects.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.870
Black holes are some of
the densest objects in

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:57.800
the universe. So we
should be able to hear

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:00.020
and measure the waves
created when they

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.840
collide. Legault.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.750
The long interferometry
gravitational wave

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:09.890
observatory listens for
waves that can come from

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:11.810
over a billion light
years away.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:20.150
In 2017, Legault heard
an enormous crash.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:25.340
Two very massive objects
collided at near the

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:28.130
speed of light. And one
of the most energetic

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:30.050
events that we have ever
witnessed in the history

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:30.920
of humankind.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:38.030
Two ultra heavy, ultra
dense objects whirl

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:40.310
around each other,
hurling powerful

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:42.650
gravitational waves
through space.

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:46.510
The closer they fall
toward each other, the

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:49.420
more gravitational
energy they throw out.

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:54.610
Finally they collide in
one of the most violent

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:56.170
events in the universe.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:02.090
The smash sends out
immense gravitational

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:07.400
waves that ripple across
intergalactic space until

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:10.220
eventually Legault
detects them.

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:15.410
Listening to a
gravitational wave is

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:18.110
like listening to a
musical instrument.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:21.440
If it's making certain
tones or certain

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:24.650
vibrations, you can
figure out the size of

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:26.630
the musical instrument,
the type of the musical

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:28.430
instrument. Who's
playing the musical

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:29.030
instrument?

